PAGES=[page('087_page_2.jpg',166,[heading('6.6 Definite Integrals'),paragraph('“A definite integral is an integral that contains start and end value, say a and b, where interval [a,b] are limits or boundaries”.'),paragraph('Look at the following figures, Figure 6.1 is showing the indefinite integral while the Figure 6.2 is showing definite integral.'),fig(1,'hump','source indefinite-integral illustration'),fig(2,'bounded-hump','area between a and b under the source curve'),paragraph('Definite integrals can be calculated in a same way as we have learnt in previous section for calculation of indefinite integral, but there is slight difference, to find definite integral simply calculate indefinite integral at point a and at point b, then subtract the result.'),note('An indefinite integral is a family of antiderivatives F(x)+C, rather than the shaded region depicted in Figure 6.1. For a definite integral evaluate one antiderivative at b minus its value at a; do not subtract in the reverse order.'),heading('Example 14'),paragraph(r'Evaluate \(\int_1^4x^2\,dx\).'),heading('Solution'),paragraph('Here a = 1,b = 4 and n = 2.'),eq(r'\int_1^4x^2\,dx=\left[\frac{x^{2+1}}{2+1}\right]_1^4+C=\left[\frac{x^3}3\right]_1^4+C'),eq(r'\int_a^bx^n\,dx=\left[\frac{x^{n+1}}{n+1}\right]_a^b+C'),paragraph('For a = 1,'),eq(r'\frac13+C','i'),paragraph('For b = 4,'),eq(r'\frac{64}3+C','ii'),paragraph('Subtract equation (i) from (ii)'),eq(r'\int_1^4x^2\,dx=\left[\frac{x^3}3\right]_1^4=\frac{64}3+C-\frac13-C=21'),heading('Do You know?'),paragraph('To calculate the approximate area of any mountain we use integration.'),note('The source writes +C beside the definite integral during intermediate steps, but a definite integral is one number; the constants cancel when the endpoints are subtracted. The power formula requires n ≠ −1 and an integrand defined/integrable over the interval.'),heading('6.6.1 Definite integral as the limit of a sum'),paragraph('This limiting process is what we mean when we say the area is the definite integral of f(x) = x² from x = 0 to x = 2. It is written symbolically as'),eq(r'A=\int_0^2x^2\,dx=\frac83','i'),paragraph('We read symbol as “the area A equals the integral from x = 0 to x = 2 of the function f(x) = x².” The number 0 is called the lower limit of integration, the number 2 is called the upper limit of integration, the function f(x) = x² is called the integrand and the dx tells us that we are integrating the function f(x) = x² with respect to the variable x.'),paragraph('If f(x) is continuous on the interval [a,b] and [a,b] is divided into n equal subintervals whose right-hand points are x₁,x₂,...,xₙ, then the definite integral of f(x) from x = a to x = b is:'),eq(r'\int_a^bf(x)\,dx=\lim_{n\to\infty}\frac{b-a}n[f(x_1)+f(x_2)+\cdots+f(x_n)]'),eq(r'=\lim_{n\to\infty}\sum_{i=1}^nf(x_i)\Delta x,\quad i=1,2,3,\ldots,n,\quad\Delta x=\frac{b-a}n','i')
]),page('088_page_1.jpg',167,[heading('Example 15'),paragraph('Find the actual area of the region bounded by the curve f(x) = x² and the x-axis in the interval [0,2].'),heading('Solution'),paragraph('For n subintervals, the width of each rectangle is'),eq(r'\Delta x=\frac{b-a}n=\frac{2-0}n=\frac2n'),paragraph('The right endpoints of the subintervals are 2/n,4/n,6/n,...,2. Substitute a = 0,b = 2,x₁ = 2/n,x₂ = 4/n,x₃ = 6/n,...,xₙ = 2n/n in equation (i) to obtain the actual area:'),eq(r'A=\int_0^2x^2\,dx=\lim_{n\to\infty}\sum_{i=1}^nf(x_i)\Delta x'),eq(r'=\lim_{n\to\infty}\Delta x[f(x_1)+f(x_2)+\cdots+f(x_n)]'),eq(r'=\lim_{n\to\infty}\frac2n[f(\frac2n)+f(\frac4n)+\cdots+f(2)]'),eq(r'=\lim_{n\to\infty}\frac2n[\frac4{n^2}+\frac{16}{n^2}+\cdots+\frac{4n^2}{n^2}],\quad f(x)=x^2,\quad4=\frac{4n^2}{n^2}'),eq(r'=\lim_{n\to\infty}\frac8{n^3}[1+2^2+3^2+\cdots+n^2]=\lim_{n\to\infty}\frac8{n^3}\left[\frac{n(n+1)(2n+1)}6\right]'),eq(r'=\lim_{n\to\infty}\frac8{n^3}\frac{2n^3+3n^2+n}6=\lim_{n\to\infty}[\frac{16}6+\frac{24}{6n}+\frac8{6n^2}]=\frac83+0+0=\frac83'),heading('6.6.2 Fundamental theorem of integral calculus'),paragraph('In previous section, we learned that we can determine the area of a region with a definite integral. However, with the tools available to us at this time, evaluating a definite integral using the summation process is rather tedious and time consuming. To provide us with a more efficient method of evaluating the definite integral, we now consider a very important theorem in calculus, the “fundamental theorem of integral calculus”. This explanation will show that the definite integral can be applied in a general manner and not only to the concept of area.'),paragraph('To help provide a better understanding of the meaning of the fundamental theorem of integral calculus, let us begin with area of a region using definite integral'),eq(r'\mathrm{Area}=\int_a^bf(x)\,dx','i'),fig(3,'area','area under a continuous nonnegative function from a to b'),fig(4,'increment','area function A(x) and added strip ΔA'),fig(5,'bounds','inscribed and circumscribed rectangle bounds on added area'),paragraph('To develop the theorem, we need to introduce a new function called the area function A(x). The function indicates the area under the graph of the function from x = a to x = b in the Figure 6.3.'),note('Use a variable endpoint for the area function: A(x) = ∫ₐˣf(t)dt. The source illustration labels the endpoint b, while the proof treats it as variable x. The min/max rectangle argument on the next page starts with a positive increment Δx.')
]),page('088_page_2.jpg',168,[paragraph('The area function A(x) is the area from a to x that must be continuous and non-negative on the interval [a,b].'),paragraph('If we increase x by Δx, then the area A(x) under the curve will increase by an amount that we call ΔA in Figure 6.4. We can see that ΔA is slightly bigger than the area of the inscribed rectangle and slightly smaller than the area of the circumscribed rectangle. In Figure 6.5, the smaller rectangle is inscribed (within the curve) and the large rectangle is circumscribed.'),paragraph('For the area of the inscribed rectangle, we take the minimum value of f(x) within the closed interval [x,x+Δx]. We call this minimum value f(m). For the area of the circumscribed rectangle, we take the maximum value within the closed interval [x,x+Δx]. We refer to this value as f(M). Hence the minimum area is f(m)Δx and the maximum area is f(M)Δx.'),paragraph('Algebraically, we can write'),eq(r'f(m)\Delta x\le\Delta A\le f(M)\Delta x'),eq(r'f(m)\le\frac{\Delta A}{\Delta x}\le f(M),\quad\Delta x\ne0','ii'),paragraph('If we take the limit as Δx → 0, then f(m) and f(M) approach the same point on the curve and both approach f(x)'),eq(r'f(x)\le\lim_{\Delta x\to0}\frac{\Delta A}{\Delta x}\le f(x)'),paragraph('which states that'),eq(r'\frac{dA}{dx}=f(x),\quad\lim_{\Delta x\to0}\frac{\Delta A}{\Delta x}=\frac{dA}{dx}','iii'),paragraph('Integrating (iii) to obtain'),eq(r'A(x)=F(x)+C','iv'),paragraph('Here F(x) is the antiderivative of f(x). To determine a real value of A(x), we must solve equation (iv) for C. Put x = a in (iv) to obtain:'),eq(r'A(a)=F(a)+C\Rightarrow0=F(a)+C,\quad A(a)=0\Rightarrow C=-F(a)'),paragraph('Put x = b in (iv) to obtain:'),eq(r'A(b)=F(b)+C\Rightarrow A(b)=F(b)-F(a),\quad C=-F(a)','v'),paragraph('The last equation (v) tells us that if it is possible to find an antiderivative of f(x), then we can evaluate the definite integral ∫ₐᵇf(x)dx. This is nicely condensed in the fundamental theorem.'),heading('Statement'),paragraph('If a function f(x) is continuous on the closed interval [a,b], then the definite integral of a function f(x) in the interval [a,b] is:'),eq(r'\int_a^bf(x)\,dx=[F(x)]_a^b=F(b)-F(a)'),heading('Proof'),paragraph('Here F(x) is any function such that F′(x) = f(x) for all x in [a,b]. It is important to recognize that the fundamental theorem of calculus describes a means for evaluating a definite integral. It does not provide us with a technique for finding the antiderivative. To find the antiderivative of a definite integral, we use the same techniques used to find the antiderivative of the indefinite integral. But what happens to the constant C? This constant C drops out as illustrated below:'),eq(r'\int_a^bf(x)\,dx=[F(x)+C]_a^b=[(F(b)+C)-(F(a)+C)]'),eq(r'=F(b)-F(a)+C-C=F(b)-F(a)','vii'),note('The rectangle bounds assume Δx > 0; the printed condition Δx ≠ 0 alone is insufficient for dividing without reversing inequalities. For a negative increment, bound the average of f on the interval [x+Δx,x] to get the same derivative by continuity. Nonnegative f is needed for this unsigned-area illustration, but the fundamental theorem itself holds for every continuous real f using signed integrals.')])]
